m op against all ops from fromRev to current\n\n### Database\n- Initialize SQLite with the schema\n- CRUD for documents\n- Version history\n\n### REST API\n- POST /api/documents - create document\n- GET /api/documents - list documents\n- GET /api/documents/:id - get document\n- PUT /api/documents/:id - update document\n- DELETE /api/documents/:id - delete document\n- GET /api/documents/:id/versions - get version history\n\n### WebSocket\n- Connect to /ws/documents/:id\n- Send/receive operations\n- Sync on connect\n\nLet me code all of this now.\n\nLet me write the files one at a time, starting with the OT engine.\n\nActually, I realize I need to think about the WebSocket protocol. Let me define the message types:\n\nServer -> Client:\n- `{ type: 'sync', content: string, version: number }` - sent on connect\n- `{ type: 'op', operation: Op, version: number }` - broadcast operation\n- `{ type: 'error', message: string }` - error message\n\nClient -> Server:\n- `{ type: 'op', operation: Op, version: number }` - send an operation based on document version\n- `{ type: 'sync' }` - request current document state\n\nOK, let me start coding. I'll write all the files.\n\nLet me also think about the `version` concept. Each document has a version number that starts at 0. Each operation increments the version by 1. The server stores operations with their version numbers in the `versions` table.\n\nWhen a client sends an operation with version N, it means \"I made this edit based on version N of the document\". The server transforms the operation against all operations from version N+1 to the current version.\n\nLet me code this now.\n\nOne more thing: the schema.sql has `active_connections` table, but I don't think I need to use it. I'll track connections in memory. Let me just use the `documents` and `versions` tables.\n\nOK, let me write the code:\n\n```typescript\n// src/ot.ts\n\nexport interface InsertOp {\n  type: 'insert';\n  pos: number;\n  text: string;\n}\n\nexport interface DeleteOp {\n  type: 'delete';\n  pos: number;\n  length: number;\n}\n\nexport type Op = InsertOp | DeleteOp;\n\nexport function applyOp(doc: string, op: Op): string {\n  if (op.type === 'insert') {\n    if (op.pos < 0 || op.pos > doc.length) return doc;\n    return doc.slice(0, op.pos) + op.text + doc.slice(op.pos);\n  } else {\n    if (op.pos < 0 || op.pos > doc.length || op.length < 0) return doc;\n    const actualLen = Math.min(op.length, doc.length - op.pos);\n    return doc.slice(0, op.pos) + doc.slice(op.pos + actualLen);\n  }\n}\n\nexport function applyOps(doc: string, ops: Op[]): string {\n  return ops.reduce((d, op) => applyOp(d, op), doc);\n}\n\n/**\n * Transform op1 against op2.\n * Returns op1' such that apply(apply(S, op2), op1') gives the correct result\n * assuming op1 and op2 were both based on the same state S.\n */\nexport function transform(op1: Op, op2: Op): Op[] {\n  if (op1.type === 'insert' && op2.type === 'insert') {\n    return [transformInsertInsert(op1, op2)];\n  }\n  if (op1.type === 'insert' && op2.type === 'delete') {\n    return [transformInsertDelete(op1, op2)];\n  }\n  if (op1.type === 'delete' && op2.type === 'insert') {\n    return transformDeleteInsert(op1, op2);\n  }\n  if (op1.type === 'delete' && op2.type === 'delete') {\n    return [transformDeleteDelete(op1, op2)];\n  }\n  return [op1];\n}\n\nfunction transformInsertInsert(op1: InsertOp, op2: InsertOp): InsertOp {\n  if (op2.pos < op1.pos) {\n    return { ...op1, pos: op1.pos + op2.text.length };\n  }\n  if (op2.pos > op1.pos) {\n    return op1;\n  }\n  // Same position: op2 was applied first, so op1 goes after\n  return { ...op1, pos: op1.pos + op2.text.length };\n}\n\nfunction transformInsertDelete(op1: InsertOp, op2: DeleteOp): InsertOp {\n  if (op1.pos <= op2.pos) {\n    return op1;\n  }\n  if (op1.pos >= op2.pos + op2.length) {\n    return { ...op1, pos: op1.pos - op2.length };\n  }\n  // Insert was inside the deleted range\n  return { ...op1, pos: op2.pos };\n}\n\nfunction transformDeleteInsert(op1: DeleteOp, op2: InsertOp): Op[] {\n  if (op2.pos <= op1.pos) {\n    return [{ ...op1, pos: op1.pos + op2.text.length }];\n  }\n  if (op2.pos >= op1.pos + op1.length) {\n    return [op1];\n  }\n  // Insert is within the delete range - split the delete around the insert\n  const beforeLen = op2.pos - op1.pos;\n  const afterLen = op1.length - beforeLen;\n  const result: Op[] = [];\n  if (beforeLen > 0) {\n    result.push({ type: 'delete', pos: op1.pos, length: beforeLen });\n  }\n  if (afterLen > 0) {\n    result.push({ type: 'delete', pos: op2.pos + op2.text.length, length: afterLen });\n  }\n  return result;\n}\n\nfunction transformDeleteDelete(op1: DeleteOp, op2: DeleteOp): Op {\n  const op1End = op1.pos + op1.length;\n  const op2End = op2.pos + op2.length;\n  \n  // op1 is entirely before op2\n  if (op1End <= op2.pos) {\n    return op1;\n  }\n  // op1 is entirely after op2\n  if (op1.pos >= op2End) {\n    return { ...op1, pos: op1.pos - op2.length };\n  }\n  // op1 is entirely within op2 (fully deleted already)\n  if (op1.pos >= op2.pos && op1End <= op2End) {\n    return { type: 'delete', pos: op2.pos, length: 0 };\n  }\n  // op2 is entirely within op1\n  if (op1.pos < op2.pos && op1End > op2End) {\n    return { ...op1, length: op1.length - op2.length };\n  }\n  // Overlap: op1 starts before op2\n  if (op1.pos < op2.pos) {\n    const overlap = op1End - op2.pos;\n    return { ...op1, length: op1.length - overlap };\n  }\n  // Overlap: op1 starts within op2\n  const overlap = op2End - op1.pos;\n  return { type: 'delete', pos: op2.pos, length: op1.length - overlap };\n}\n\n/**\n * Transform an operation against a sequence of operations.\n */\nexport function transformAgainstHistory(op: Op, history: Op[], fromRev: number): Op[] {\n  let ops: Op[] = [op];\n  for (let i = fromRev; i < history.length; i++) {\n    const newOps: Op[] = [];\n    for (const o of ops) {\n      newOps.push(...transform(o, history[i]));\n    }\n    ops = newOps;\n  }\n  return ops;\n}\n```\n\nWait, there's an issue with the transform function returning Op[]. When I broadcast to other clients, I need to send the transformed operation. If it's been split into multiple ops, I need to send all of them.\n\nBut when a client receives a broadcast operation and transforms its pending operation against it, it needs to handle each sub-operation individually.\n\nLet me think about the client-side transform. In our architecture:\n1. Client sends an operation with a revision number\n2. Server transforms it and applies it\n3. Server broadcasts the transformed operation to other clients\n4. Other clients receive the operation and apply it to their local document\n\nBut what about the client's own pending operations? In a simple model, the client waits for the server to acknowledge its operation before sending the next one. This is simpler to implement.\n\nFor the server, I need to:\n1. Accept operations with revision numbers\n2. Transform against concurrent operations\n3. Apply and store\n4. Broadcast (including the revision number)\n\nLet me also think about the revision numbering. The document starts at version 0. Each operation creates a new version. So after operation 1, the document is at version 1. The `versions` table stores the content at each version.\n\nActually, let me re-read the schema:\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS versions (\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n  document_id TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES documents(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  version INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  content TEXT NOT NULL,\n  operation TEXT, -- JSON of the operation that produced this version\n  created_at TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (datetime('now')),\n  UNIQUE(document_id, version)\n);\n```\n\nSo `version` starts at 1 (or 0?). Let me say version 0 is the initial state (empty or initial content), and each operation increments the version. The `versions` table stores the content after each operation.\n\nLet me also think about what happens when a docum